<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="no" ?>
<FinalDraft DocumentType="Script" Template="No" Version="5">
<Content>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. THE MAGNOLIA - REAR LOT - FIRST LIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Steam lifts off black runoff. A charged hose line lies across wet gravel.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The rear door hangs open. Just inside it, beneath the panic bar, a length of chain lies where it was cut. The padlock is still locked through two links.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Five people lie together near the door. What can be seen of them from here: shoes, a sleeve, a hand turned palm up. No faces.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. THE MAGNOLIA - REAR HALL - CONTINUOUS</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Water drips. Somewhere ahead, the drone of fans.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A mop sink. Empty crates along one wall. No sign marks the hall or the door.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The storeroom threshold. Daylight comes straight down through the open roof onto black shelving and foam stock burned into itself. On a deep sill under the high window, glass has slumped and set.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. THE MAGNOLIA - BARROOM - CONTINUOUS</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The hall opens into the barroom from the back. A soot line runs the walls, waist high here, climbing toward the front. Below it, the room is intact.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Bottles ranked behind the bar under a string of dead Christmas lights. A drum kit and a guitar on the bandstand. Tables shoved aside with chairs stacked on them, a coaster still folded under one leg. A purse hangs from a chair back. On another table, a longneck, half full.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Three feet inside the front doors, in the light from the lot, the many lie together, all facing the doors. Backs. Shoulders. Hair. Past them the fans roar in the doorway.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. THE MAGNOLIA - FRONT LOT - CONTINUOUS</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Daylight. Down the road, a siren, pulling away.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The tape. Behind it, the crowd that stayed all night: blankets over shoulders, a child asleep against a leg, backs to the first light. Firefighters coil hose on the gravel. One sleeps sitting up on a bumper.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Apart from all of them, a man in a hospital scrub top. Fresh white gauze wraps both palms. He holds his hands away from his body. He is looking at the doors.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>From the front, once: his face.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He turns and walks off through the crowd. No one makes room for him.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The ground where he stood. Wet gravel. Nothing on it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>HOLD.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The crowd noise drains away. The fans. The siren.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>BLACK.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. HOYT HOUSE - KENDRA&#x27;S ROOM - EVENING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>SUPER: EARLIER. HALE RIVER, ALABAMA. 2014.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A small room, the window unit laboring. Tops on the bed, three deep. BRIANNA, 16, round-faced and sure of herself, her hair done and her shoes already on, sits cross-legged on the bed with Kendra&#x27;s phone. KENDRA HOYT, 16, her father&#x27;s long frame already, headphones around her neck, holds a top against herself in the mirror on the closet door.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KENDRA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Or this.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>BRIANNA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That&#x27;s a church top.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KENDRA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It is not a church top. I wore it to the thing.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>BRIANNA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You wore it to church and then to the thing.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Kendra throws it on the bed with the others and goes back into the closet. Brianna goes back to the phone.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>BRIANNA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Marcus is going.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KENDRA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Who said.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>BRIANNA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Marcus asked Jalen was Darnell going, and Jalen asked Darnell, and Darnell said he&#x27;d go if Marcus was going, and Jalen told Marcus that, and Marcus said then he&#x27;d go. So they&#x27;re both going. Because of each other.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Kendra comes out with a third top and holds it against herself in the mirror. Brianna is behind her in it, still on the phone.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KENDRA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He sits in front of me third period and every time she passes papers back he turns all the way around. He&#x27;s got nothing to say. He just turns around.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Brianna puts the phone down.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>BRIANNA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He&#x27;s looking at you.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KENDRA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He&#x27;s looking at Tasha.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>BRIANNA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Tasha sits by the door.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KENDRA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>This one.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>BRIANNA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That one. Did your daddy say he&#x27;s driving?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Kendra changes into it. The next lines come through fabric.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KENDRA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He&#x27;s driving. She can call Miss Ondine.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>BRIANNA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>My mama called Miss Ondine. Miss Ondine talked to her forty-five minutes about her hip.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KENDRA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Which hip.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>BRIANNA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I don&#x27;t know which hip, Kendra.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The front door, off. Boots on the floor. Brianna is off the bed before Kendra is, and hands her the phone on the way past.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT (O.S.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Kendra.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KENDRA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>In here.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. HOYT HOUSE - KITCHEN - CONTINUOUS</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>AMOS HOYT, 40s, Black, lean, in a work shirt with the sleeves rolled, sets a toolbox down inside the door and goes to the sink for his hands. Brianna sits at the table without being asked. Kendra stops in the doorway in the new top.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>BRIANNA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Hi, Mr. Hoyt.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Brianna.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He turns with the towel and sees the two of them. The top.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>What are y&#x27;all into.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KENDRA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Nothing.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That&#x27;s a lot of nothing.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KENDRA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The movies. Everybody&#x27;s going.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Who&#x27;s everybody.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KENDRA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Everybody.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>BRIANNA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Tasha, and Darnell, and Marcus, and --</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KENDRA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Everybody.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Marcus.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He opens the refrigerator and stands in it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That&#x27;s the Shelby boy. I put a belt on his mama&#x27;s dryer. He stood in that laundry room the whole time I was down on the floor and never once handed me a thing. I&#x27;d say hand me that, and he&#x27;d look at it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He takes out the tea, pours a glass, and sits down across from Brianna, the first time he has sat since he came in.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I might come.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KENDRA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>No.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I&#x27;ll sit in the back. I&#x27;ll sit with Marcus. Hand him some popcorn, see does he take it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Brianna laughs. Kendra doesn&#x27;t, and then does.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He gets up with the tea and heads for the hall. In the doorway he turns back and looks at the top again.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You going in that?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KENDRA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>What&#x27;s wrong with it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Nothing&#x27;s wrong with it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He looks at it a second longer than that.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Then somebody better start supper. I ain&#x27;t driving nobody hungry.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Kendra is already at the refrigerator before he has finished saying it. He goes down the hall. Brianna stays where she is at the table, like she lives there.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. ALDRIDGE DRY GOODS BUILDING - REAR - NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Dark windows. Closed loading door. No traffic on the block.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A BICYCLE comes down the alley and stops. The rider is small, a canvas bag across his back. His face stays out of the light.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He lays the bicycle in the weeds. Packing straw and baled dry goods crowd a timber post against the rear wall. A pocket knife starts a strip of cotton sheeting; bare hands tear and twist it tight.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He kneels at the timber post, clears away the loose straw and places the twisted cotton between the post and the lowest bale. He pushes the straw back around it, leaving one end exposed.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A lighter. He cups the flame and touches it to the exposed cotton.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The flame travels down the twist and disappears beneath the straw.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He closes the lighter. He lifts the bicycle out of the weeds and rides to the far end of the block. He stops with one foot on the ground and looks back at the building.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Smoke slowly thickens around the timber post.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>From the end of the block, the boy watches the smoke build.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. RUCKER HOUSE - KITCHEN - NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A brick ranch kitchen with the table in it. Dark outside the window over the sink. Supper on the table, two plates going. ELDON RUCKER, 46, tall and square-built, white shirt open at the collar, is on his feet at the head of the table with a fork in his hand he has forgotten about. TY, 16, broad- shouldered like his father and not yet grown into it, a boy&#x27;s face on a man&#x27;s frame, eats with his head down. The chair at the other end is pushed back and empty. We come in on the middle of it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>-- the truck&#x27;s in up to the door handles. Door handles. And I go in after the cooler. And your mama&#x27;s shoes.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The cooler floated. It come out the window on its own and floated right to you.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I got the cooler.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The carport door. DONNA, 44, small and straight-backed, her hair pinned off her neck against the heat, quick on her feet, comes in with a gallon of tea from the other refrigerator. She heard the one word she needed to.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DONNA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You were on the phone.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I was coming down the dock.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DONNA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You were on the dock on the phone with Arlen Moss, telling me left with your hand.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She sets the tea down and does it, a phone at one ear and the other hand waving.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DONNA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>And when it started rolling you reached in the window past me and put it in neutral. On a ramp.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That&#x27;s the first thing they teach you, you take it out of --</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DONNA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>On a ramp, Eldon. It rolled faster.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I got the cooler.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Ty laughs with his mouth full. Rucker sits. Donna sits in the empty chair and pulls her plate to her.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>For a while, plates. The window unit in the next room.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DONNA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Tell your daddy what Coach said.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He didn&#x27;t say anything.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DONNA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He said if you keep --</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He said if I keep doing what I&#x27;m doing. He says that to everybody, that&#x27;s not -- he said that to Darnell and Darnell can&#x27;t --</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Who&#x27;s in front of you.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Terrell.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Terrell&#x27;s a senior. Seniors graduate.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He goes back to his plate and says the rest of it into the beans.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It ain&#x27;t if. It&#x27;s when. When you start, I&#x27;m there.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Okay.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Ty eats. Donna is looking at Ty, not at Rucker.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DONNA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You said that about the banquet.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The banquet was a --</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DONNA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I know what it was. I&#x27;m saying you said it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He stops. Puts the fork down.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Ty.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Ty looks up.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I&#x27;m telling you now so you can hold me to it. When you start, I&#x27;m in the stands.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Okay.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Donna gets up and takes her plate and Ty&#x27;s. Ty gets up with the glasses. Rucker&#x27;s phone BUZZES in his shirt pocket. He looks at Donna before he takes it out. She has her hands full.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Rucker.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He listens.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Where.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He listens. Ty sets the glasses by the sink and stays there.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That&#x27;s the old dry goods. Who&#x27;s got it? What&#x27;s he -- no. No, I know. Anybody in it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He listens.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>All right. Call me when it&#x27;s knocked down. I&#x27;ll be there at daylight.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He sets the phone on the table face up beside his plate.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DONNA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Where.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Aldridge.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DONNA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Eat your supper.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He picks the fork back up. Ty is looking at the phone, and then at his father, who is looking at the phone.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. ALDRIDGE DRY GOODS BUILDING - NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Through a facepiece. The edges of the lens frame everything. Breathing, close, already going.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Gloved hands come up on both sides of the lens and press. A thumb runs the seal. Behind the hands, masked, CAPTAIN CARL SLOAN, 50s, barrel-chested, gray mustache. The man inside the facepiece is DANNY MERCER, 25, probationary firefighter, broad-shouldered, young.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SLOAN</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(muffled, mask)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Air?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MERCER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(muffled, mask)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Full.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Sloan puts the regulator to the mask and it clicks home. He takes Mercer&#x27;s shoulder strap in his fist and turns him toward the building.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A two-story red-brick building on a block of shuttered storefronts. Smoke pulses from the eaves. No flame visible. An ENGINE at the corner, EARL DOBBS, 40s, company engineer, at the pump panel looking up at the roofline. Ladders going to the roof. The RIT team waiting with tools.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>At the command board, the INCIDENT COMMANDER, radio traffic in both ears.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INCIDENT COMMANDER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(into radio)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Command to all units, building reported vacant, occupancy unconfirmed. Engine Three, you&#x27;re interior. Give me conditions when you&#x27;ve got them.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Sloan&#x27;s hand comes off the strap long enough to point at the hose. The door. The dark past it. They get low and go in.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. ALDRIDGE DRY GOODS BUILDING - CONTINUOUS</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>BLACK.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Breathing. Close, mechanical, air pulled through a regulator, pushed back out.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Water moving. A pump throbbing far off, through walls.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A helmet lamp comes up: light that reaches three feet.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Gloved hands on a hoseline, following it forward. The line is the only road in here. Back along it is the door.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The lamp finds the nozzle. Mercer braces behind it. Sloan keeps one hand on his shoulder strap.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>They move on their hands and knees. Heat keeps them there.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SLOAN</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(muffled, mask)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Pencil it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Mercer opens the nozzle at the overhead, one short burst. It comes back down on them as hissing steam. They keep moving.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MERCER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Where is it?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SLOAN</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Above us or ahead of us. Watch your ceiling.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Sloan keys the radio on his chest.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SLOAN</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(radio)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Heavy smoke. High heat. Zero visibility. We&#x27;re penciling.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INCIDENT COMMANDER (V.O.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(radio, tinny)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Interior, you in position with a charged line?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SLOAN</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(radio)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Charged and on it. It&#x27;s above us. Give us a hole and we&#x27;ll take it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. ALDRIDGE DRY GOODS BUILDING - NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Three firefighters sound the roof ahead of every step with pike poles.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INCIDENT COMMANDER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(into radio)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Roof, Command. Open it up.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. ALDRIDGE - ROOF - CONTINUOUS</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The saw starts on the third pull. It revs to full power. The blade bites hard into the roof.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Smoke presses along the cut.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The opening breaks through. Smoke stands up in a hard column.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. ALDRIDGE - CONTINUOUS</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The smoke LIFTS a foot off the floor.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The building appears as the draft takes hold: shelving rows. A dead forklift. An aisle. For the first time, a shape to the dark.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SLOAN</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>There&#x27;s our vent. Move.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>They advance up the aisle, the line paying out behind them.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Outside, the engine pump climbs higher, Dobbs feeding them more pressure.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Ahead, the ceiling GLOWS through its seams.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Fire rolls from the ceiling void, across the overhead, running for the vent. Above the roof the cut throws flame, and the roof crew goes flat.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Behind Sloan, a section of ceiling and steel rack lets go. CRASHES across the hoseline.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The lamp beams disappear into black.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SLOAN</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Back it out! Back --</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The hose line is pinned beneath the debris. Sloan hauls at it. Mercer is three feet deeper into the room, cut off from Sloan by burning shelving. Three feet.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MERCER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Cap --</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The VIBRALERT goes off against his jaw. Low air.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SLOAN</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(radio)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>MAYDAY MAYDAY MAYDAY. Engine Three. Firefighter separated. Northeast corner. Low air --</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INCIDENT COMMANDER (V.O.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(radio, tinny, far)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Command copies the Mayday. RIT is deploying.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Mercer, alone.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He drops a glove onto the hoseline and follows it. The line goes two ways. One way is the nozzle. The other is the door.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Five feet. A coupling. His fingers find the lugs on the far side. Lugs point to the nozzle. Wrong way. He has been crawling deeper in. He turns and crawls back along the line.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The regulator gives him less air. The Vibralert pounds, slows, then stops. He inhales and gets nothing; the facepiece seal pulls tight against his cheeks.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>His gloves grip the edges of the facepiece.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He does not pull it off. He releases it and puts both hands back on the hoseline.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He crawls. Slower now. Each attempt to inhale pulls the seal tighter. His helmet light drops toward the floor.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He stops moving.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The beam remains fixed across the floor. Smoke passes through it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>His PASS device begins to chirp. The warning accelerates, then rises into a full electronic SHRIEK.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Nothing else moves. Faint traffic comes through the radio on his chest:</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SLOAN (V.O.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(radio, tinny)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Mercer. Mercer, key your mic.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Nothing. The shriek. Smoke crossing the beam.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RIT OFFICER (V.O.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(radio)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>RIT to Command, we&#x27;re at the collapse. No contact. We&#x27;re digging.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INCIDENT COMMANDER (V.O.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(radio)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Command to all units, emergency traffic only. Three minutes on the Mayday.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The beam is dimmer. The smoke is thick and rolling.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SLOAN (V.O.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(radio, raw)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He was right beside me. He&#x27;s on the line. He is ON the line --</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INCIDENT COMMANDER (V.O.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(radio)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>RIT, Command. Status.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Static. No answer.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The beam. The smoke. The shriek.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A GLOVED HAND reaches through the smoke, finds Mercer&#x27;s shoulder strap and locks on.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RIT FIREFIGHTER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(muffled, close)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>GOT HIM! We got him! GO!</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Mercer is YANKED backward. Floorboards rush beneath him. His lamp swings away from the beam.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. ALDRIDGE - ENTRY DOOR - CONTINUOUS</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Two RIT firefighters drag Mercer across the threshold. His mask is still sealed. His PASS continues to scream until one of them shuts it off.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Clean air. Concrete apron. Mask off. Helmet off.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A FIREFIGHTER-MEDIC&#x27;s fingers go to his throat.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FIREFIGHTER-MEDIC</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>No pulse. Starting compressions.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Coat open. Compressions. Bag-valve mask. AED pads. Behind them, the building ROARS as master streams open up.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Mercer COUGHS, wet, tearing. Fights the mask on his face.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FIREFIGHTER-MEDIC</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Pulse. We got a pulse. Package him.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He keys the mic on his shoulder.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FIREFIGHTER-MEDIC</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Command, Medical. I want the helicopter.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>COMMAND (V.O.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Say again, Medical.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FIREFIGHTER-MEDIC</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Helicopter. He was down and he&#x27;s back. We need to get him to the nearest trauma center.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. ALDRIDGE - CLEARING - LATER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Open ground a hundred yards off the fire. The aircraft comes up off the grass with Mercer inside it. Rotor wash drives ash flat across the lot. The firefighters under it turn their backs.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>It noses over the tree line. Gone.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. ALDRIDGE DRY GOODS BUILDING - MORNING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Only steam rises from the building now. Yellow tape closes the block.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Crews pull hose lines back toward the engines. Pike poles shoved into compartments. A firefighter removes his SCBA and sits on the bumper.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A white STATE FIRE MARSHAL TAHOE noses to the curb. State seal on the door. Low-profile light bar. Nothing else announcing it. Out of it: RUCKER. Clean white shirt.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>From the rear hatch: boots, windbreaker, camera, clipboard, Maglite.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Sloan approaches. Rucker keeps his eyes on the building: steam at the eaves, a blackened window, roof sag, open doors.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Your boy?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Sloan falls in beside him.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SLOAN</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Breathing. They&#x27;re keeping him the night. His mama got there about four.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Rucker hears that. He doesn&#x27;t say anything for a moment.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Good.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Nothing else, for a moment more.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Anybody inside since overhaul?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SLOAN</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Chief walked it about an hour ago. Ain&#x27;t nobody been in it since.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>At the tape, Rucker stops for one look, roofline, windows, cornice, entry, ground, then ducks beneath it. Sloan follows.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. ALDRIDGE - BURNED SHELL - MORNING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Wet black. Daylight coming down through the roof cut. Water dripping off everything.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Rucker enters, stops. Scans the room, then walks the main aisle, slow. Puts the light on a wall and follows a smoke line down it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(to the room)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>All right.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He crouches at a burned-out rack. Runs a thumb through char, then checks its depth with a small gauge from his shirt pocket.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That&#x27;s the water. That&#x27;s not you.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He puts the beam up into the truss, then along the ceiling to the roof cut and the daylight standing in it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Down the aisle. Bolts of cloth on their sides, burned to the core. A rack of cotton ticking. Packing straw everywhere, gone to gray.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He moves down the aisle taking readings, photographing the floor clean here, cooked there, his light held low and raking.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He arrives at the REAR CORNER. Low burn, deep char, climbing out of a heap of what used to be stacked stock and packing straw. He crouches. Stays down.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>There you are.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He lays the beam along the base of the wall and follows the burn where it runs the joint.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He puts the light up the wall. Bare block. No box, no conduit, no fixture.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>No box. No cord. No lamp.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He lifts a length of collapsed shelving with a pen, looks under it, sets it back where it was. He works the corner with the light. Turns over a piece of burned strapping with the pen.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Sat here half the night. No can. No splash. Used what they left.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He lays the beam flat across the floor. Follows it into the heap.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Under the packing straw: a STRIP OF COTTON, twisted tight before it burned. Still holding its twist.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Rucker doesn&#x27;t touch it. Photographs it where it lies.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Same as Cedar Street.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He studies the twist.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Same hand.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. STATE FIRE MARSHAL - FIELD OFFICE, HALE RIVER - DAY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A storefront office. A scanner murmurs from the next room.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>On the long wall, a county map. Three pins. Cedar Street, the mill store, Aldridge. Dates beneath them in a careful hand.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Rucker stands at his desk, phone to his ear. His camera sits open beside him. He wipes soot from the lens with the corner of a cloth while he listens.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(into phone)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>No, sir. Not kids. Kids burn what&#x27;s handy. They burn it where they&#x27;re standing. This one&#x27;s picked three buildings nobody&#x27;s had a use for in years. And he waits.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He listens.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(into phone)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Yes, sir. I&#x27;d keep that word off the radio a while longer.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He hangs up. The cloth stays in his hand. He walks to the map. Studies it. Not the pins. The roads between them. The rivers. The abandoned mill properties.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>His finger hovers over a crossroads, then lowers.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. THE MAGNOLIA LOUNGE - DAY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>In through the front doors. Chairs up on the tables. Daylight across a floor that&#x27;s seen a Saturday. Christmas lights over the bar, off.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>On the bandstand, JUNE HOLLIS, 23, small, dark-haired, runs a song alone at the mic, half-voice, the way you sing a thing you already know to find out where it goes wrong. Even at half-voice there is more in it than the room needs. A BUZZ under her in the PA.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Hoyt comes out from behind the bandstand with a cable in his hand. Reseats it. The buzz dies.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JUNE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You fix amps now?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I fix whatever&#x27;s broke before Friday.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He gathers the slack out of the cable. She taps the mic, listens to it, sings two notes into it and stops.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JUNE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It&#x27;s flat.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It&#x27;s fixed.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JUNE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Not the buzz. Me. I&#x27;m flat on the --</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Sounded fine to me.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JUNE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You weren&#x27;t listening.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I was under the stage.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JUNE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That&#x27;s what I said.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She goes again from the top. Hoyt stays to listen.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Under the song, DEENA CALLOWAY, 40s, lean, handsome, fast on her feet, a pencil behind her ear, comes out of the kitchen patting her apron. Pats the bar. Checks her jacket on the hook. Looks at the register, and the shelf under it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Hoyt watches her do all of it. Then holds up her keys.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Walk-in. In the lock. Again.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DEENA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I was coming right back for them.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Third Friday running you were coming right back.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DEENA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You counting?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Somebody in this building better be.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She takes the keys and swats him with her bar rag. Hoyt picks up his toolbox and goes toward the back. June is still singing.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. DOT&#x27;S DINER - DAY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Lunch thinned out. Two booths still working on pie. A ceiling fan that moves the smell of the grill around and nothing else.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Behind the counter, DOT MABRY, 60s, flat-footed, forearms built by decades of handling trays. She pours before she&#x27;s asked to.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>In the back booth, RUCKER. Hat on the seat beside him. A file open. Cold cup of coffee in front of him. Dot tops it off without breaking his concentration.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>MERCER comes in, civilian clothes, a hospital bracelet still on his wrist, and stops inside the door the way a man does in a place he has never been. He spots Rucker. Crosses to the booth and stops a step short of it, hands at his sides.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MERCER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Marshal. Captain Sloan said I&#x27;d find you here.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Rucker turns a page.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MERCER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Marshal.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I heard you.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He reads to the end of something. Then his eyes come off the page as far as the hospital bracelet, and no farther.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You&#x27;re his probie.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MERCER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Danny Mercer.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>They tell me you kept your facepiece sealed.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MERCER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Yes, sir. Academy said keep the seal. Better to go into arrest than breathe the room. That&#x27;s what the -- my mama asked the nurse what arrest meant and the nurse said --</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He stops, because that is not what he was asked.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Said what.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MERCER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Said it meant my heart stopped. She already knew that. She just wanted somebody to say it to her.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Rucker lets that go by. He says the next thing to the page.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Well. That&#x27;s why you&#x27;re standing in Dot&#x27;s and not laying out at Shady Grove.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Mercer shifts his weight. He has nowhere to put his hands and leaves them where they are.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The ceiling in that building. Tell me the last thing it did before it came down.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MERCER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It came down. It let go all at once --</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Before that.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Rucker waits with his file. He doesn&#x27;t look up.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Mercer looks at the table, not at him. At the cold cup.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MERCER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It got quiet. Rolling and popping the whole way in, then it got quiet.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Rucker closes the file. Looks at Mercer for the first time. Pushes the chair out with his boot.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Fire in a void goes quiet right before it shows itself. You had four seconds of quiet. You spent them moving. Sit down.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Mercer sits on the front of the chair, sore, the bracelet arm held a little off the table. Dot passes with the pot. Without asking, she leaves a fresh cup beside Mercer. He starts to thank her. She&#x27;s already at the next booth.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>They tell me you can&#x27;t pull hose for six weeks.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MERCER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Doctor said six. Chief said we&#x27;ll see.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>They also tell me you lost the line and your captain had to put a Mayday out on you.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MERCER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Yes, sir.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Rucker lets that sit on him. Looks at the bracelet. At the file under his own hand. He does not like what he is about to do.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Can you carry a camera without losing it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MERCER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Yes, sir.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Then here&#x27;s how it goes. You drive mine. You carry what I hand you. You don&#x27;t touch anything I haven&#x27;t pointed at. And the main thing, you listen twice as much as you talk. You start now.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MERCER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Now.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Rucker doesn&#x27;t answer that. He slides the file across the table, turns it so it reads right side up to Mercer. Cedar Street photographs.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Mercer looks at the file, not at him. Puts a hand flat on the edge of it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>At the front, a check paid, a purse closed. MISS ADELE FANNIN, 80s, stops beside the booth on her way out, because it is his booth and she always does. One hand flat on the table, the other on her purse. She looks at the boy, and at the bracelet, a while.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MISS ADELE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>This your boy, Eldon?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Sloan&#x27;s.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MISS ADELE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Hm.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She has decided something about him anyway. Then she looks at the file.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MISS ADELE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Eldon.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Rucker looks at Mercer, not at her. Opens the Cedar Street file to the first photograph and turns it toward him.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Yes, ma&#x27;am.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Miss Adele pats the table once and heads for the door. The bell jingles. Dot lifts the pot at her without stopping.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Mercer studies the first photograph, then the next. The bracelet goes under the edge of the table.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Rucker drinks the cold coffee and watches him.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. STATE FIRE MARSHAL - FIELD OFFICE, HALE RIVER - NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Two desks pushed together under the county map. One lamp between them. The scanner in the next room, turned down.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>MERCER at the second desk, the Cedar Street file open in front of him. No bracelet now. He reads each page to the bottom before he turns it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Under the lamp, evidence photographs from Cedar Street and Aldridge lie side by side. In each, a strip of burned cotton still holds its twist.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Rucker puts the loupe over one, then the other, then back again.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(to the photographs)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Did you bring these with you, or did you find them there?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MERCER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Sir?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Rucker doesn&#x27;t look up.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Him. If he found it in the building, it tells me about the building. If he carried it in, it tells me about him.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Mercer comes over with the file still in his hand. He studies both photographs. He doesn&#x27;t touch them.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MERCER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>They&#x27;re the same cloth.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>They&#x27;re both cotton.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MERCER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>No. Same cloth. Look at the -- this side, both of them. Cut clean on one side and pulled on the other. Somebody cut off something big and tore the rest by hand.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Rucker moves the loupe to the torn edge. Looks at what Mercer saw. Stays there.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Sheeting.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MERCER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Mill sheeting. My grandmother had forty yards of it. Every pillowcase in that house. Curtains. She was still cutting dish rags off it when I was --</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He sees Rucker hasn&#x27;t come off the loupe. He lets it go.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Rucker takes the loupe off. Sits back.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It&#x27;s in half the shop rags in the county. There&#x27;s a piece of it in my own truck box.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He squares the photographs, one over the other.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>So he didn&#x27;t have to go far for it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MERCER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Nobody would.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Then I&#x27;ve narrowed it down.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He returns the photographs to the folder. Looks up at the map. Three pins. Then blank wall where the next pin goes.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Mercer goes back to his desk, and to the file. Rucker stays on the map.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A page, read to the bottom. Another.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MERCER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You ever ride an engine?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Eleven years.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MERCER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>What made you get off?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Rucker doesn&#x27;t turn from the map.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Put out four hundred fires and couldn&#x27;t have told you what started three of them. Got to where I couldn&#x27;t stand it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MERCER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Right. Right. And now you know.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Now I find out.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Mercer turns a page.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MERCER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Chief asked when I&#x27;m coming back on the engine.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>What&#x27;d you tell him?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MERCER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Six weeks, like the doctor said.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Rucker comes off the map and sits. Pulls the folder to him and doesn&#x27;t open it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A page. Another.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MERCER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I didn&#x27;t tell him I don&#x27;t miss it. Figured I would. On that floor I wasn&#x27;t thinking about the engine. I was thinking I never once knew where the fire was. Not one time. And this --</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He doesn&#x27;t finish it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Rucker looks at him. He doesn&#x27;t say anything.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Mercer waits for it. Then stops waiting and goes back to the page.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Rucker opens the folder.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MERCER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Your wife mind the hours?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Ask her. Twenty years.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Mercer turns the page. Then turns it back, and reads it again.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. COUNTY ROAD - DAY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The state Tahoe moves along two-lane blacktop, fields on either side.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>MERCER rides in the passenger seat with a short stack of thin folders on his knee. The top one is a county juvenile referral form.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The Tahoe turns in at a small frame house off the blacktop.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A bicycle lies on its side in the yard, half in the tall grass. Rucker parks beside it and looks at it. They get out.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>They cross the yard. At the porch, Rucker stops with his hand short of the screen door.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>This is the part nobody makes me do.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Mercer looks at the top folder in his hands. The referral form.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MERCER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The grass fire.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Sixteen years old. I&#x27;d like that to be the last one he lights.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He knocks.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. SMALL FRAME HOUSE - KITCHEN - DAY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A clean, tired kitchen. A dish towel of old mill sheeting hangs on the oven door. THE BOY, 16, sits at the table in a T-shirt, eyes down. His MOTHER, 40s, arms folded, stands at the counter. Mercer stands inside the door with the folders.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Rucker sets the juvenile folder on the table in front of the boy. Squares it to the edge. The boy&#x27;s name on the tab, facing him. He leaves it closed, sits down across from him and puts his hat on his knee.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The boy looks at the folder. His mother looks at it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That grass caught quick behind the school. Dry as it&#x27;s been.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Nothing from the boy.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MOTHER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Vernon. Look at the man.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He doesn&#x27;t.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Ma&#x27;am. It&#x27;s all right. That&#x27;s why it&#x27;s me sitting here and not a deputy.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He takes a blank contact card from his shirt pocket.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(to the boy)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Station One runs a junior program. Thursday nights.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He writes his name and number on the card.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>They&#x27;ll let you pull hose. You want to be around fire, that&#x27;s where it&#x27;s allowed.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He sets the card beside the folder.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Any other night you feel it coming on, you call me. I answer.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Nothing from the boy. The card stays where he put it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He puts two fingers on the folder.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(to the mother)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He shows up Thursdays, this stays closed.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>His mother is looking at Vernon, not at him.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Rucker puts on his hat. Mercer follows him out. The screen door claps shut behind them. The card stays on the table between the boy and his mother.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Vernon gets up from the table and goes to the window over the sink. A curtain on a string, pushed to one side the way it always is. He stands there.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Outside, the Tahoe backs out to the blacktop and pulls away.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Vernon watches it go.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. MILL DISTRICT STREET - NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Three in the morning. A dog barking. Porch lights come on down a street of small frame houses.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Beyond the rooftops, smoke rises, lit orange from below. Ash falls through the streetlight.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Screen doors open. Families gather on their lawns in bathrobes and unlaced boots. A man carries photo albums from his house to his truck.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Sirens, converging on each other.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. THE OLD LINE HALL - NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A hundred-year-old, two-story heavy-timber building. Flames are already through the roof as the first engine pulls up. SLOAN comes off the rig.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SLOAN</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Hall&#x27;s gone. Get lines on the houses. Both sides.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Crews pull lines toward the neighboring houses. An aerial ladder goes up. Its master stream sweeps from one roof to the next.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Wind carries embers across the street. A firefighter sweeps a hose stream through them.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Residents gather behind the tape. Bathrobes, work jackets, children on shoulders. They watch in near silence.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>JUNE runs up in a coat over her nightclothes. She stops at a charged hose line crossing the street. Across it, the hall burns. A neighbor puts a hand on her shoulder.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>At the edge of the crowd, Rucker&#x27;s Tahoe, driver&#x27;s door hanging open.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>RUCKER stands at the tape with his back half to the fire. He watches the faces.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Mercer arrives beside him, camera in hand. He&#x27;s watching Sloan&#x27;s crew haul line toward the houses. His crew.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MERCER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I&#x27;d be on that line.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Last one, you were on the floor.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MERCER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>What do we do?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Not the fire. Them. Every face you can get.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Mercer turns the lens on the crowd and starts shooting.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Back from the tape, among the bathrobes, Vernon stands with his hands in his pockets and watches the water hit the roof. Not the fire. The water.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Mercer&#x27;s lens passes across him with the rest.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The hall&#x27;s roof collapses inward, sending sparks above the building. People behind the tape cry out.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>June stands beside the hose line, her arms at her sides. Orange firelight on her face.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MERCER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You think he&#x27;s here?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Keep shooting.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. VFW HALL - NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Folding chairs, every one full. Standing room at both doors. Phones up and filming. Beside the entrance: a coffee can with a slot cut into the lid and a hand-lettered sign-up sheet: NIGHT PATROL.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>FIRE CHIEF ARLEN MOSS, 50s, thick through the middle, turnout pants under a department polo, and POLICE CHIEF ROY PARDUE, 50s, small and neat, hair combed wet, wait at the front without a stage or podium. RUCKER enters with MERCER.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PARDUE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I got four men and two cars. They go home scared, half this town&#x27;s walking their street with a shotgun by Friday.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Then let&#x27;s give them something to do that ain&#x27;t that.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Rucker hands his hat to Mercer, who moves to the side wall. Rucker walks to the front and waits. A chair scrapes. Someone lowers a phone. The last conversation stops.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Evening. Eldon Rucker. Deputy state fire marshal. I&#x27;ll stay till the questions run out.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A WOMAN, 30s, is on her feet before he&#x27;s finished.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>WOMAN AT MEETING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>My kids sleep upstairs. What are we supposed to do at night?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Sleep.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A beat.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That part&#x27;s mine.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The woman sits. The room settles.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Four fires. Same hand. Three empty buildings, then the hall. All four empty when he lit them. He looks before he lights, and he&#x27;s looked the same way four times running.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A MAN by the door, 40s, work jacket, not waiting on a hand:</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MAN AT DOOR</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Is he from here? Is it somebody we --</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A SECOND WOMAN, 60s, is up near the back and already over him.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SECOND WOMAN</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The hall wasn&#x27;t but a block from houses. What if he stops being careful?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Rucker takes hers.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He&#x27;s had four.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A beat.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He won&#x27;t get five.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Nobody moves.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Porch lights on. Sign that sheet if you want to walk with a deputy. You see something, call it in. You do not follow it, and you do not carry a gun looking for it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>An OLD MAN takes the patrol sign-up sheet and passes it down the first row. Rucker answers questions until no hands remain raised. On his way out, he puts several folded bills into the coffee can.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Outside, two men lock their rifles in separate trucks. Mercer returns Rucker&#x27;s hat. Rucker watches the room empty.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>If he was in that room, he didn&#x27;t come for answers.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. THE MAGNOLIA - FRONT - NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Late. The jukebox comes through the door. JUNE on the front step, apron still on, a cigarette going, phone to her ear.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PRODUCER (V.O.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(phone)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>...so I played it for Marty Keel. He runs A and R over there. He loved it. He wants to see you work a room, so he&#x27;s coming to you. Friday.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The cigarette stops halfway to her mouth.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JUNE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Coming here.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PRODUCER (V.O.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Flying into Birmingham, driving down. He&#x27;ll sit in the back and he&#x27;ll watch. If it goes the way I think it goes, he&#x27;ll want breakfast with you Saturday before he heads back. Might. I&#x27;m saying might.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JUNE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>What&#x27;s he want to hear?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PRODUCER (V.O.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Marty? Whatever you&#x27;d do anyway. Don&#x27;t build him a set. Then you fly Tuesday, I&#x27;ve got you Wednesday and Thursday, two days, we cut the two.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JUNE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Tuesday.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She takes a pen from behind her ear and writes on the back of her hand. SAT, with a question mark after it. TUES under that.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PRODUCER (V.O.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Go sing. I&#x27;ll send the flight.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He&#x27;s gone. The cigarette has gone out in her fingers. She looks at it, and at her hand, and goes in.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. THE MAGNOLIA LOUNGE - CONTINUOUS</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Kitchen closed, the pass dark. Three tables still going. The jukebox.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>At the end of the bar, DEENA, shoes half off under the stool, counts her tips into stacks on a bar towel. June comes straight to her.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JUNE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That was him.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DEENA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Hold on. Thirty-five. Forty.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JUNE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Marty Keel&#x27;s coming Friday. Here. To hear me --</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DEENA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Who?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JUNE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>From the label. He&#x27;s flying in to --</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DEENA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Now I&#x27;m at thirty-five again.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She starts the stack over.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DEENA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>So you got a record.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JUNE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I got two days in a studio and a plane ticket.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DEENA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>And then?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JUNE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>And then I&#x27;m there.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DEENA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>There where.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JUNE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Out there. I&#x27;m not coming back to the trays, Deena, I&#x27;m --</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DEENA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>What you do is, you get it in writing before you --</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JUNE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>There&#x27;s no deal.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Deena stops. Squares the stack she isn&#x27;t counting.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DEENA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You don&#x27;t know anybody out there.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JUNE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I don&#x27;t know anybody here I didn&#x27;t grow up with.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Deena goes back to the stack without counting it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DEENA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Merle hasn&#x27;t put the sign up. For your shifts.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JUNE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He thinks I&#x27;ll change my mind.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DEENA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Will you?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JUNE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>No.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DEENA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Then let him watch.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JUNE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Merle?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DEENA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Your Marty. Let him watch.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She folds the towel over the stacks and works her shoes back on.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DEENA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Forty.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JUNE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I told you forty.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DEENA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You told me nothing. You talked over it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>June laughs, and Deena does, and it goes over the jukebox and down the back hall, where the service door stands propped open to the lot.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. THE MAGNOLIA - REAR DOOR - NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Late. The lot is nearly empty. The rear door stands propped open on a brick, kitchen light across the pavement.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>MERLE COBB, 60s, owner of the Magnolia, reading glasses pushed up in his hair, on the phone beside the door. A hardware-store bag at his feet.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>COBB</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(into phone)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>-- no, bring it in the back. The back. ... Monday. Monday&#x27;s fine. ... I said it&#x27;s fine. Tell Dunphy.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He hangs up.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>BIG PETE TILLMAN, 50s, six-four, heavy through the chest and belly, thick hands, comes out the propped door onto the step on his round, and stops when he sees Cobb.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>COBB</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You hear what&#x27;s going on in town?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>BIG PETE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>June?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>COBB</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>No. Somebody&#x27;s burning buildings.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He picks up the bag and hands it to Pete.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>COBB</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Nobody comes through my back door.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Cobb goes in. Pete follows him.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. THE MAGNOLIA - REAR HALL - CONTINUOUS</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Cobb kicks the brick out and lets the door swing shut behind them. Latched. The push bar across it at waist height.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Pete takes a length of chain and a padlock out of the bag, the price sticker still on it. He looks at the door. Then at the push bar.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>BIG PETE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That&#x27;s an exit, Merle.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>COBB</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It&#x27;s a service door. Ain&#x27;t nobody using that at night.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>BIG PETE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It&#x27;s got a bar on it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Cobb says nothing.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Pete holds up the chain.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>BIG PETE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Are we even allowed? I don&#x27;t think we can chain that door.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>COBB</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I ain&#x27;t risking somebody walking in here and burning this place down in the middle of the night.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>From the barroom, down the hall, a burst of laughter. Music underneath it. Pete looks that way.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>BIG PETE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Every night?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>COBB</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Every night. Once the kitchen&#x27;s closed.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Pete runs the chain through the push bar and the door frame and snugs it tight. He closes the padlock.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>SNAP.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Cobb takes the key and threads it onto his key ring. Pete glances at the chained door.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Music, voices and bottles clinking come down the hall.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Cobb pockets the key ring.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. THE MAGNOLIA - REAR SERVICE AREA - DAY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The rear kitchen door stands open. Inside, afternoon prep: pans, glass racks across counters, the walk-in door opening and closing.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Outside, the walk-in&#x27;s CONDENSING UNIT runs with a loud, uneven hum. HOYT kneels beside it with the access panel off. Meter leads clipped inside. The screws lined up on a folded shop towel.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The unit shuts off. Hoyt checks his watch.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>COBB steps out through the rear door.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Thirty-six seconds.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The unit kicks back on.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>COBB</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Amos. Leave that a minute.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Hoyt listens to the restart, then gets to his feet. Wrench in hand.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>COBB</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Insurance won&#x27;t renew us without sprinklers. Dunphy&#x27;s bid is the whole mechanical, and I can&#x27;t carry that note and keep you both.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Wait... What? When?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>COBB</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Today.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Hoyt unclips the meter leads and coils one around his hand.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>When&#x27;d you give Dunphy the work?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>COBB</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Last week.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Nine years.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>COBB</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I know. I know what I owe you. Three Sundays, two weeks on top. Cash on the fifteenth.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That ain&#x27;t what I said.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The unit shuts off again. Hoyt checks his watch. He stops with the meter lead half-coiled around his hand.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Part comes Tuesday.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>COBB</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Dunphy can handle it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Hoyt looks through the open rear door, across the kitchen and into the dim rear hall.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He don&#x27;t know what that motor sounds like when it&#x27;s right.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>COBB</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He&#x27;ll learn.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He&#x27;ll learn what breaks. I learned what keeps it from breaking.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Inside, a glass rack slows across the counter.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Nine years, Cobb. Every storm. Every time something quit after midnight, you never had to wonder if I&#x27;d come.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>COBB</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I know what you did.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>No. You know what you paid me for.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Cobb has no answer.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The glass rack has stopped. The KITCHEN HAND holding it, 20s, a kid in a wet apron, watches through the open door. Cobb notices.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>COBB</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Lower your voice.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Hoyt doesn&#x27;t.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You gave him my building before you gave me the truth.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>COBB</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It isn&#x27;t your building.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Been mine every time it broke. You keep waiting till everything breaks.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>COBB</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That&#x27;s enough.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Hoyt throws the wrench. Not at Cobb. It hits the block wall beside the door and rings off the pavement. Inside, the kitchen stops.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>One morning you&#x27;re gonna come in here and find this whole place gone.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The last pan settles into the sink. Nothing moves in the kitchen.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>COBB</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It ain&#x27;t anything you did.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I know.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The glass rack starts moving again. Prep resumes, quieter.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Hoyt goes and picks up the wrench. Kneels beside the unit and fits the access panel back into place.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>COBB</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You can leave that.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Can&#x27;t leave it open.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He runs in the first screw.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Ordered that part off my account.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>COBB</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Then finish this one. After that, bring me the keys.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I&#x27;ll get it in.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Cobb goes back through the kitchen. The unit kicks on.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Hoyt listens to it working.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He runs in the rest of the screws. Puts the meter away, gathers his tools, picks up the toolbox and the shop towel.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. THE MAGNOLIA - STOREROOM - CONTINUOUS</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Hoyt comes in off the rear hall with the toolbox and the towel. The walk-in cooler&#x27;s compressor sits against the wall with its access panel off, a loose component resting on a rag beside it. He sets the toolbox down, settles on the floor beside the component and goes back to cleaning it with degreaser. Steel shelving. A high west window above it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A CREW MAN, 30s, in a Dunphy T-shirt gone gray at the collar, backs in through the louvered door with a roll of renovation foam on his shoulder and swings it down against the steel shelving, beside two already there.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Hey. Don&#x27;t stack that against my shelving.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The crew man looks at the roll. Then at the man on the floor. He leaves the roll where it is.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>CREW MAN</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Boss told me get it out of the barroom.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Hoyt goes back to the component.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>CREW MAN</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Who are you?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Your boss don&#x27;t work back here.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The crew man goes out for the next one, comes back with it, and sets it against the first.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>CREW MAN</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I don&#x27;t know what to tell you, man. There&#x27;s more coming Friday.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The compressor kicks on, runs rough, then settles. Hoyt listens to it. Then he gets up, takes a length of wire off a shelf, and ties the louvered door open to a shelf bracket while the crew man works around him.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Door stays open. Thing&#x27;s got to breathe.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The crew man ducks the wire on his way out. Says nothing.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A five-gallon glass water jug, a carboy, with a couple inches of water sits in the path. On the way back in with the next roll, the crew man&#x27;s boot misses it by inches. Hoyt lifts the jug onto the deep sill beneath the high window, out of the way, and goes back down to the floor. Caps the degreaser. Puts it on the shelf.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>At the doorway, Cobb raises a cheap digital camera and takes a wide photograph for the renovation insurance file. The whole west wall fits in the frame, and the man on the floor under it. Neither of them says anything. Cobb moves on to the next room.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. THE MAGNOLIA - REAR LOT - CONTINUOUS</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Hoyt walks past the renovation trucks carrying his toolbox. He places it in the bed of his pickup and climbs into the cab.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He closes the door. The noise of the lot falls to a distant clatter.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Hoyt sits behind the wheel without starting the engine.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Through the windshield, workers unload lengths of sprinkler pipe. Beyond them, the Magnolia&#x27;s open rear door.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He watches a moment longer, then takes the building keys from his pocket. He finds the worn brass front-door key and starts working it around the split ring.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>His phone BUZZES.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The screen reads JUNE.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Hoyt closes his hand around the keys and answers.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>June.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JUNE (V.O.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>-- Friday. He&#x27;s coming Friday, Amos. He&#x27;s flying in. To hear me live, in a room, with people in it, not off a tape, he wants to see what I --</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Who is.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JUNE (V.O.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Marty Keel. From the label. The label, Amos, I told you about the -- I didn&#x27;t tell you. I told Deena. Okay. There&#x27;s a producer out in Los Angeles who heard the tape, and he played it for --</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He let me go, June.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Nothing on the line.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Hoyt looks at the front-door key, halfway off the ring. His thumb presses against its edge.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JUNE (V.O.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Since when.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Since ten minutes ago.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JUNE (V.O.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Nine years, Amos.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It ain&#x27;t him. It&#x27;s the sprinklers, he says.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JUNE (V.O.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Friday&#x27;s my last show.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Might not be welcome now.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JUNE (V.O.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Come anyway.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Hoyt watches the workers carry the sprinkler pipe through the door.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>What time you start?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He works the front-door key back onto the ring.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Hoyt puts the truck key in the ignition and starts the engine.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. HOYT HOUSE - EVENING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The small frame house at the end of the short street. The work truck in the yard, hood up. The window unit drips onto bare dirt.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>KENDRA holds a droplight into the engine bay, headphones down around her neck. Her left hand&#x27;s nails are painted. The right one, holding the light, isn&#x27;t. On the fender, among the sockets and the rag: a coil of baling wire.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>HOYT is in past his elbows.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Light.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She moves it two inches.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Other way.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She moves it back to where it started. He works in it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KENDRA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You could hold it and I could do the wrench part.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You could.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He keeps working. She keeps holding.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Start it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She gets behind the wheel and starts it. The engine runs, a fast tick under the idle.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>What do you hear?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KENDRA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The truck.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Under it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She closes her eyes. Listens.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KENDRA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Ticking. Little fast ticking, like a sewing machine.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Hoyt nods once and draws a hand across his throat. She kills the engine.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Lifter. Needs oil more than it needs us.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He wipes his hands on the rag.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Can&#x27;t fix a noise you can&#x27;t name.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She comes back around to the fender and takes up the light without being told.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KENDRA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Trish&#x27;s after church Sunday?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Sunday&#x27;s Sunday.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He pulls the dipstick. Reads it against the last of the daylight.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KENDRA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You&#x27;re home before supper. All week you&#x27;ve been home before supper.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A moment. Hoyt seats the dipstick back in its tube.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Hold the light.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She looks at him a second. Then holds the light. He goes back in past his elbows.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KENDRA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I get my license in March.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Truck&#x27;ll be ready.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KENDRA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>March, Daddy.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I heard you.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He works another moment. Then he straightens, wipes the wrench on the rag and holds it out to her. Kendra looks at it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Trade.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She hands him the light and takes the wrench. He pulls a length off the coil of baling wire, twists it into a loop around the droplight&#x27;s hook, and hangs the light from the raised hood over the engine bay. Kendra leans in over the fender, both arms inside up to her elbows.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Tell me where.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KENDRA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Other way.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Hoyt shifts the light two inches on its loop. Kendra smiles down into the engine and keeps working.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. THE MAGNOLIA LOUNGE - NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Warm, loud, low-ceilinged. Christmas lights over the bar in July. A dance floor the size of a kitchen. A four-piece band on a bandstand one foot high.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Pete on the door, calling every name coming in.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Deena lands four longnecks on a table mid-story and keeps moving.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>JUNE comes in off the front step past Pete, tucking a pack into her apron pocket. Pete&#x27;s look follows her. She drops a basket at a table, then steps up onto the bandstand still in her apron and unclips it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DEENA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(as she goes by)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>One of these days you&#x27;re gonna have to pick. Tray or that guitar.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JUNE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I picked. This is the last tray.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DEENA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Listen to her.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>At a corner table, RAY BEASLEY, 70s, lean in a pressed shirt, holds his hat on one knee while ONDINE, 70s, in a floral dress, talks with both hands and straightens his collar.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>At the bar, a bottled Coke sweats before an empty stool. LACEY STROUD, 17, slight, bright red lipstick and a church dress taken in at the waist, slips in behind a birthday crowd and claims it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A man comes in alone. The only sport coat in the room. Pete holds the door for him and has no name to call. The man goes to the bar and stands at the back of the crowd with the wall behind him, where he can see all of it. This is MARTY KEEL.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Deena, going by with a tray, looks at him a second longer than a stranger gets. Keeps going.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>HOYT enters in a clean button-down, empty-handed. Pete&#x27;s handshake holds an extra beat.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>BIG PETE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You eat?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>She on yet?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Pete lets go of his hand. Hoyt goes in.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Passing a rocking table, Hoyt crouches and folds a coaster beneath one leg. As he rises, Ondine catches him by the arm.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ONDINE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Merle ought to be ashamed. Nine years, Amos.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Ain&#x27;t my place anymore.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ONDINE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It ain&#x27;t right, Amos.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I came for June.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She lets him go. At the end of the bar, Deena sets a beer in front of him without being asked. The band finishes tuning. The drummer counts it in before June has the strap over her shoulder. The count dies. The room quiets.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>June looks once to the back of the room. A man she doesn&#x27;t know, at the bar, looking back. She doesn&#x27;t look there again.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JUNE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(at the mic)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Ray and Miss Ondine. Miss Ondine has talked me through every bad day I ever had, and Ray sat right there and let her. Thirty-one years married, tonight. This one&#x27;s theirs. And that man still won&#x27;t dance.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RAY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(from his table)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Can&#x27;t. Not won&#x27;t.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>People at the tables laugh. Her boot comes down, one, two, and the band hits. A slow number.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Ondine looks at Ray. Ray holds his hat.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>June sings. Full voice now.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The floor. Couples who have been here before, and two who haven&#x27;t, finding the step. A table by the wall stops talking in the middle of something. In the kitchen door, the kid in the wet apron with a bus tub on one hip, not moving.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Lacey on her stool, the Coke in both hands, watching the bandstand.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Hoyt at the end of the bar. The beer where Deena put it. He watches June.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>At the back of the crowd, Marty. He has stopped looking at the room and is looking at her. Deena works the bar in front of him, pulling longnecks for a tray.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARTY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Does she know they&#x27;re here for her?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Deena racks the bottles. Doesn&#x27;t answer. He waits, then gives it up and turns back to the floor. To June.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DEENA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(over his shoulder)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>She thinks they come because it&#x27;s Friday. She always has.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He doesn&#x27;t turn around. He is smiling.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>At the door, Pete looks across the packed floor. People are still coming.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>BIG PETE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(half to himself)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Ain&#x27;t even payday.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Nobody answers. The floor fills.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>June, eyes closed on the long note. Then open, on them. The couples. Ondine&#x27;s hand on Ray&#x27;s hat. The whole room facing one way.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. LAKE CABIN - NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Rucker&#x27;s pickup stops beside a dark cabin above the water. DONNA comes around the front of the truck with her hand out.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DONNA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Danny&#x27;s covering the tip line. You told me that Tuesday. You told Danny that Tuesday.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He takes the phone out of his shirt pocket and puts it in her palm.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DONNA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Glovebox till Sunday.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Yes ma&#x27;am.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She leans in through the passenger door and shuts it in the glovebox. He gets the rods.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. LAKE CABIN - DOCK - NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A lantern on the dock. Still water. Crickets.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Rucker and Donna in folding chairs with their lines out. A thermos between them. Her bare feet on the cooler.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>For a while, only the water.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DONNA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>First time you brought me here, you talked the whole way up.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Road was washed out at Hensley Creek.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DONNA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>See. I had my shoes off and my feet up on your dash, and you were telling me where the county kept its grader.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You had your feet on my dash.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DONNA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You noticed that.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He lays his rod on the planks, reaches across the thermos and takes her hand.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I noticed.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She turns her hand over in his.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DONNA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I thought you were showing me the country.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Rucker watches his float.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I was nervous.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Donna turns toward him.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DONNA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You never told me that.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You knew.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DONNA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I knew something. I didn&#x27;t know whether you meant to marry me or inspect the road.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Both worked out.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She pours coffee into the thermos cup and hands it to him.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DONNA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Twenty years.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Twenty.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DONNA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Still nervous?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Rucker takes the cup.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Not about you.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Donna settles back in her chair. Her feet go back up on the cooler.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DONNA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Ty walked a girl out to the truck after practice.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Rucker looks over.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DONNA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I was in the window. He had the door open for her and he was talking before she got to it. The defense. Who&#x27;s starting. Every bit of it. He never said one word about her.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>What girl.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DONNA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>She stood there with her books and let him tell her about Terrell.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Rucker is laughing before she&#x27;s done.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Did she get in the truck?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DONNA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>She got in the truck.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He laughs at the water. She lets him.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Who is she?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DONNA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You know her. The tall one, with the --</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Headlights come through the trees above the cabin and cross the water.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She stops.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>UP AT THE CABIN</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The truck sits dark. Inside, a phone BUZZES in the glovebox, light flashing twice through the seam.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A county cruiser noses down the road and stops above the cabin.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>BACK ON THE DOCK</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Rucker is already standing.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A DEPUTY comes only as far as the top of the dock. He takes off his hat and holds it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>LAKE DEPUTY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Marshal.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Where.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>LAKE DEPUTY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Magnolia.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Rucker picks up his hat from the chair.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Behind him, Donna puts out the lantern and folds the chairs.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The cruiser heads back up the lake road. Rucker&#x27;s truck follows.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The fishing rods remain on the dock.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Two lines still in the water.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. RUCKER&#x27;S TRUCK - NIGHT (MOVING)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Two-lane blacktop at speed. Over the trees ahead, a glow spreads across the sky.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Donna sits beside Rucker with his jacket over her shoulders. The phone is out of the glovebox, face up in the console between them. Neither speaks.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The phone rings. Rucker thumbs it onto speaker without taking his eyes off the road.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Rucker.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>COE (V.O.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(speaker)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Where are you?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Fifteen out. What are you hearing?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>COE (V.O.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Juke joint called the Magnolia, west of town. Friday night, better than a hundred inside when it lit. They&#x27;re still carrying people out the front, and command just went defensive.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Donna looks at the phone.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Who all&#x27;s working it?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>COE (V.O.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>ATF&#x27;s got an investigator rolling from Atlanta. Origin and cause stay with you.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>All right.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>COE (V.O.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Call me when you&#x27;ve stood in it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The line goes dead. The glow is brighter.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DONNA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Ty needs the truck at seven.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Not now, Donna.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She looks at him.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DONNA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I didn&#x27;t ask you anything.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Rucker keeps his eyes on the road.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>No. You didn&#x27;t.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A moment.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I&#x27;m sorry.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DONNA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I know.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The road bends. Rucker takes the curve.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>His hat slides across the seat between them. Donna catches it before it reaches the floor and holds it in her lap.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. THE MAGNOLIA - NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Flames break through the rear section of the roof. The high windows glow orange. Smoke pushes from the eaves and rolls across the parking lot.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Fire apparatus fill the lot and line both sides of the road, Hale River companies and mutual-aid crews from across the county. Aerial ladders extend above the building. Supply lines cross the road.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INCIDENT COMMANDER (V.O.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(radio)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Command to all units, defensive operations. Surround and drown.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>At the front doors, the last hose crew backs out low through the steam, heads down, the line still charged. Their officer counts them past him and waves the next crew off the doorway.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>MASTER STREAMS arc onto the roof from three sides.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>THE FRONT LOT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Survivors line the curb, barefoot and soot-streaked beneath blankets. A deputy moves among them with a clipboard, taking names.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Several feet apart from the rest, LACEY stands barefoot in an oversized jacket, watching the front doors.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>No one comes out of them.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>THE REAR SERVICE AREA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>COBB comes around the corner of the building in his nightclothes, headed for the back. A firefighter catches him by the arm and turns him toward the road.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Past the firefighter&#x27;s shoulder, in the light off the apparatus: the rear service door hangs open, and the severed chain lies across its threshold, the padlock still locked through two links.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Cobb sees it. The firefighter walks him away from it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>AT THE TAPE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Several hundred residents watch, the woman from the VFW meeting among them. No phones are raised.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Inside the tape, MERCER photographs the crowd. Approaching headlights make him lower the camera.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Down the mill road, beyond the last engine, a pickup sits dark on the shoulder, a red gas can beside its tank.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>RUCKER&#x27;S TRUCK clears the roadblock and stops near the tape. Through the windshield, Donna in the passenger seat with his hat in her lap. Rucker gets out, walks to the line and studies the building from roof to foundation.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He does not move.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RUCKER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(to a deputy, quiet)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Who&#x27;s out?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FRONT-LOT DEPUTY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Working on it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Mercer raises the camera to the crowd again.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>At the edge of his frame, AMOS HOYT stands alone in a hospital scrub top, fresh white bandages around both palms, his hands held away from his body. He is watching the flames vent through the rear roof.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>CLICK.</Text></Paragraph>
</Content>
<TitlePage><Content><Paragraph Alignment="Center"><Text>FLASHOVER</Text></Paragraph><Paragraph Alignment="Center"><Text>RUNNING MASTER, PART ONE, PART TWO AND 3.1</Text></Paragraph><Paragraph Alignment="Center"><Text>Restructure, 22 October 2026</Text></Paragraph><Paragraph Alignment="Center"><Text>Working assembly. Locked: Hoyt household; 1.2 Aldridge; 2.2 Dot; 2.7 the chain; 2.12 Friday. All else NOT ADOPTED.</Text></Paragraph></Content></TitlePage>
</FinalDraft>
